{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>The autosave worked for months, then one write silently stopped saving anything again</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A notes app autosaves a draft to <code>localStorage</code> on every keystroke, one <code>setItem</code> call, no bounds on how much history piles up over time. It works in every test, because test sessions are short and drafts are small. Months into real use, a long-time user's stored draft history finally crosses the browser's storage limit for that site, and the very next <code>setItem</code> call throws.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing catches it. The throw happens inside the keystroke handler, so the rest of that handler, including the code that would normally show "Draft saved," never runs either. Autosave doesn't show an error. It just quietly stops working, and the user finds out days later when their browser crashes and the last hour of notes isn't there.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      <code>localStorage.setItem()</code> is not guaranteed to succeed. Every browser enforces a real per-origin quota, typically 5&ndash;10MB depending on the browser, and once a write would exceed it, <code>setItem</code> throws synchronously, a <code>QuotaExceededError</code> <code>DOMException</code>, it doesn't truncate the value or silently drop it. Code that calls <code>setItem</code> without a <code>try/catch</code> is one long-lived user session away from an uncaught exception in whatever handler happened to call it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The failure mode compounds because it's rarely reproducible locally. QA sessions are short-lived and start from a clean profile, so the quota is never remotely close to being hit; the bug only shows up for real users after weeks of accumulated writes, or for anyone whose browser applies a smaller quota than the developer's, private browsing windows in some browsers have historically set the quota to zero, meaning the very first write throws, not the thousandth.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>The quota is real, the throw is synchronous, and localStorage isn't what "clear cookies" clears</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      <code>localStorage</code> gives each origin a shared bucket, typically 5&ndash;10MB total, for every key it ever writes, combined. Once a write would push the total past that limit, the browser doesn't shrink the value or ignore the call, it throws an exception right there, synchronously, and whatever called <code>setItem</code> needs to be ready to catch it. Handling it well means wrapping writes in <code>try/catch</code> and having an actual fallback ready: delete some older entries first, keep only the most important data, or fall back to holding that particular write in memory for the rest of the session instead of crashing.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      There's a separate, non-obvious gap most users (and plenty of engineers) don't expect: clearing cookies, the button most users reach for to "reset a site," does not reliably clear <code>localStorage</code>. It's a different storage bucket with its own settings surface, so data written months ago can outlive several rounds of a user "clearing everything" through the cookie-focused UI they actually know about.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Storage partitioning adds another layer worth knowing: modern browsers increasingly partition storage per top-level site to block cross-site tracking (Safari's Intelligent Tracking Prevention, Firefox's Enhanced Tracking Protection), so the same origin embedded as a third-party iframe on different sites gets separate, isolated <code>localStorage</code> instances rather than one shared bucket, data written in one embedding context is invisible in another. And some private/incognito browsing implementations have set the effective quota to zero rather than disabling <code>localStorage</code> outright, which means <code>setItem</code> is reachable and throws immediately, indistinguishable from a real quota-exceeded error unless the code is written to treat both the same way.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>It shipped clean, then broke for every user on a private-browsing window</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team shipped a "recently viewed items" feature that appended to a <code>localStorage</code> array on every page view, unbounded, no cap on how many entries it kept. It worked in every review and every demo. A batch of support tickets started arriving from users saying the app "wouldn't load" or "crashed instantly," and every one of them turned out to be using a private/incognito window.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      In the private-browsing configuration those users hit, the browser's effective <code>localStorage</code> quota for the session was zero. The very first <code>setItem</code> call in the app's init path, writing an empty default state, threw immediately. Because that call sat in a critical startup function with no <code>try/catch</code>, the exception propagated up and stopped the app's initial render entirely, a real quota edge case took down the whole page, not just the recently-viewed feature.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The fix treated every <code>localStorage</code> write in the app as fallible by construction: a small wrapper function that tries <code>setItem</code>, and on any thrown error, whether from quota, private-browsing restrictions, or storage being disabled by policy, falls back to an in-memory <code>Map</code> for that session instead of propagating. The recently-viewed feature itself got a bound: cap at 50 entries, evict the oldest on overflow, so it would no longer be the thing eventually tripping the quota for long-lived users even once the crash-on-write bug was fixed.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

