{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Treat every write as fallible, and know what actually resets this data</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      The simplest durable fix is a small wrapper around every <code>localStorage.setItem</code> call that catches any thrown error and falls back to doing nothing worse than "this particular write didn't persist," rather than letting the exception escape into whatever handler triggered it. Chrome DevTools can simulate a smaller storage quota (Application panel → Storage) specifically so this fallback path can be tested without waiting weeks for real data to accumulate.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A real eviction strategy needs more than "catch and ignore": track roughly how much is stored (or just cap the number of entries outright) and evict the oldest, or least useful, entries before writing new data, so the app degrades gracefully at the boundary instead of intermittently failing writes forever once it's near the limit. <code>sessionStorage</code> shares the same synchronous, string-only, quota-bound API, it doesn't solve this problem, it just adds "also cleared when the tab closes" on top of it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      For data that genuinely needs to survive indefinitely and at real scale, IndexedDB is the better-suited API, it doesn't remove the need for error handling, but its quota is typically far larger and it wasn't designed as a small synchronous key-value scratchpad. For localStorage specifically, keep in mind that the browser's "clear browsing data" flows generally do offer a path to clear it (usually filed under "site data" or "cookies and other site data," not just "cookies"), but that path is opt-in and separate from what most users think of as "clearing cookies," so don't rely on it as an implicit reset mechanism, and don't store anything in localStorage a user would reasonably expect to disappear when they clear cookies from the simpler, more visible control.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

